<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Canton of Lucerne</h1><p class="st-meta">1332 CE – 1798 CE · capital Lucerne</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first city to ally with the Swiss forest cantons, in 1332, and later the leading Catholic power of the Confederacy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lucerne grew around the monastery of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leodegar">Leodegar</a> where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reuss-river">Reuss</a> leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lucerne">Lake Lucerne</a>, and boomed after the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard</a> route in the 13th century. Sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1291, the town allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-uri">Canton of Uri</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-schwyz">Canton of Schwyz</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-unterwalden">Canton of Unterwalden</a> in 1332, and the Swiss victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Sempach</a> in 1386 freed it from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> rule.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A small council of patrician families governed the city and a large rural territory acquired in the 15th century. Lucerne lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard</a> transit trade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> contracts. It led the Catholic cantons during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, hosted the papal nuncio from 1586, and with its allies crushed the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-peasant-war-of-1653">Peasants' War</a> of 1653.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The patrician regime abdicated in 1798 as French troops invaded, and Lucerne briefly served as capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. Restored as a canton in 1803, it remains a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1332 CE – 1798 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.21%;width:9.27%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1332 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lucerne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swiss German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Switzerland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lucerne, Luzern</dd></dl></aside></div>
